From d29a62010d405713b40df555441810c106aa56e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Orsavio P. Delmare" <orsavio@delmare.lu>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 18:49:32 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 40/50] leds: add the IPQ807x LEDC driver, and wire up the
 ECW336's five LEDs

The ECW336 drives all five of its LEDs (power, lan, 2.4/5/6 GHz) from the
IPQ807x LEDC block over a serial clk/data/clr interface on GPIO 18/19/20, not
from GPIOs -- so with no driver for "qti,ledc" the board booted with no LED
indication at all, and the gpio-leds the sibling dtsi provides (GPIO 54/55/56)
are not this board's LEDs; they appear nowhere in its DTB.

kmod-leds-ipq-ledc is derived from the QSDK driver drivers/leds/leds-ipq.c
(ISC). The register model is kept verbatim -- twelve CG registers at
base + 0x20040 initialised from qti,tcsr_ledc_values, brightness as one bit in
CG6 at the position named by qti,ledc_blink_indices[i], blink as a byte field
(enable|freq|duty) in CG9 -- with these changes:

  - .remove returns void (>= 6.11) and devm handles ioremap/LED registration,
    so the vendor's error unwinding goes away;
  - LEDs register via led_classdev_register_ext() with led_init_data from each
    child node, so the DT decides the sysfs name. The vendor driver formatted
    "ipq::led%d" into a *stack* buffer and left cdev.name pointing at it after
    probe returned;
  - module_platform_driver() rather than module_platform_driver_probe(), so
    deferred probe works while pinctrl comes up;
  - the unused exported helpers are dropped;
  - it maps with devm_ioremap() instead of requesting the region, because the
    LEDC registers sit inside the TCSR block that the hwspinlock driver
    already owns (01905000-01924fff : 1905000.hwlock) -- requesting it fails
    with -EBUSY, which is why the vendor used a bare ioremap() too.

Every DT value is the vendor's own, read out of the DTB the stock firmware
boots: the twelve TCSR values, the blink indices (CG6 bit positions, ordered
power, lan, 2.4G, 5G, 6G) and the LED-index/blink-source pair. The pinmux
carries over unchanged because mainline's pinctrl-ipq8074 has the same
led0/led1/led2 functions on GPIO 18/19/20.

On hardware: "leds-ipq-ledc 191e000.ledc: registered 5 LEDs", all five appear
in /sys/class/leds, brightness and the timer trigger both drive the registers,
and led-boot/led-running now resolve to the power LED again.
